From a9e4d3e5698be399edc0aee8fa8e7cfd896694a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:11:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] feat(control-plane): verify plugin signatures and grants
 at registration

PluginManager.register stored any manifest object as it was: nothing
checked that the artifact or the manifest came from a trusted author,
and check_permissions treated the permissions a manifest requested as
already granted, so a plugin could give itself platform.admin by
listing it.

register now re-validates the manifest (a model mutated after
construction or built with model_construct is rejected, and the record
keeps the validated copy) and verifies the detached Ed25519 signature
against the trusted keys of a plugin policy. Both digests are
recomputed from the artifact content and the manifest, so a tampered
artifact, a manifest changed after signing, an unsupported algorithm,
an untrusted key or a forged signature all raise PluginRejectedError
and nothing is registered. Unsigned plugins are only accepted when the
manager is constructed with dev_mode_allow_unsigned=True; the mode is
off by default, logs a warning, and still verifies signed plugins.

Manifest permissions are now requests. A plugin's effective permissions
are its requests intersected with the PluginGrant an admin configured
for its metadata.id, and check_permissions decides by those; names
outside the Permission vocabulary raise ValueError instead of quietly
returning False.

The policy file ({"trustedKeys": [...], "grants": [...]}) is validated
strictly: invalid or duplicate keys, duplicate grants, versioned plugin
ids and unknown permissions are refused. It is decoded with json.loads
before validation because model_validate_json silently drops keys that
are spelled like the Python field names (trusted_keys), which would
turn a typo into an empty key list.

Breaking: register() needs the artifact content and its signature
unless development mode is on, and permissions a manifest requests no
longer take effect without a grant. PluginManager is only used by
library callers and tests today; the control-plane app does not
construct one, so there is no new environment variable.
